Dear All Things Additive Editorial Team,

Solidon3D 0.3.0 has been released. The desktop application takes an imported
model through practical geometry changes, printability analysis, physical
material assignment and external slicer handoff while keeping the project
local. It also constructs new printable parts from dimensions, sketches and
tested components rather than presenting itself as a full CAD replacement.

The clearest example begins with an STL whose mounting hole is wrong. Solidon
recognises the existing cylindrical feature, preserves its position and axis,
and changes the diameter as a reversible operation. A standards-derived
insert, screw clearance or bearing seat can then be placed using fit values
measured from a printed calibration artefact.

Each physical filament spool carries its own colour, temperatures, cooling,
retraction and material properties. The application writes those values into
the correct 3MF material slot and maps them into the slicer handoff, avoiding
the common failure where one colour inherits another material’s process data.

Since the first 0.1 demo, 220 curated user-visible changes have added
in-viewport constrained sketching, exact-body tools, reusable parametric parts
made from operation history, printable matching fasteners and stronger
feature tracking. Solidon runs on Windows, macOS and Linux without a customer
account, telemetry or cloud project store. Its optional LLM layer can request
registered tools but cannot compute geometry or execute generated code.

